{"generated":"2026-10-26T10:27:08.576Z","entries":[{"id":"flippy-bit","kind":"deck","name":"Flippy Bit","description":"One card that's a whole arcade game. Invaders fall carrying a hex number (B7, 3C, F0…) and you flip eight bits with the keys 1 to 8 until they spell it, which shoots the invader down. It keeps speeding up until one lands. The deck runs on the drill scheduler, so there's nothing to rate: play as many runs as you want and Stats charts invaders per run, seconds per invader and how clean your flips were. Fun way to make hex to binary automatic. After Q42's Flippy Bit and the Attack of the Hexadecimals from Base 16. Uses the Flippy Bit card type, which comes along.","tags":["binary","hex","game","computer science"],"cover":"assets/market/covers/deck-flippy-bit.webp","items":1},{"id":"periodic-table","kind":"deck","name":"Periodic table","description":"All 118 elements, each asked eight ways, one subdeck per way: type the symbol, type the name, tap its cell on a blank table, name a lit-up cell, give its atomic number, period and group, its atomic mass, its family and block, and write its electron configuration. Every answer ends on the element's tile and its place in the coloured table, so the table builds itself in your head as you go. Uses the Element card type, which comes along. Data from PubChem.","tags":["chemistry","periodic-table","science"],"cover":"assets/market/covers/deck-periodic-table.webp","items":944},{"id":"piano-chord","kind":"type","name":"Build the chord","description":"Play a chord on a two-octave piano. A new root and quality every review; any voicing or inversion counts, graded by pitch classes.","tags":["music","piano"],"cover":"assets/market/covers/type-piano-chord.webp","items":1},{"id":"catppuccin-frappe","kind":"theme","name":"Catppuccin Frappé","description":"The softest dark Catppuccin: muted pastels on a dusky blue-grey base, with a mauve accent. Palette by Catppuccin (catppuccin.com), MIT.","tags":["catppuccin","pastel","da